# Drawing a curve with pgfplots from a data file

You can use `\addplot table` to read data from a file. Here are the main approaches:

## Option 1: Single column data (y-values only)

If your `data` file contains only y-values (one per line):

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{pgfplots}

\begin{document}

\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}
  \addplot table [x expr=\coordindex, y index=0] {data};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}

\end{document}
```

The `x expr=\coordindex` automatically generates x-coordinates starting from 0 (or 1 if you prefer, use `x expr=\coordindex+1`).

## Option 2: Two column data

If your file has x and y values:

```latex
\addplot table [x index=0, y index=1] {data};
```

## Example

**data file content:**
```
1.5
2.3
3.1
2.8
4.2
```

**Complete LaTeX code:**
```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{pgfplots}

\begin{document}

\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  xlabel=X,
  ylabel=Y,
  title=My Curve
]
  \addplot table [x expr=\coordindex, y index=0] {data};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}

\end{document}
```

This will plot points at (0,1.5), (1,2.3), (2,3.1), (3,2.8), (4,4.2).

## Common options

- `x expr=\coordindex+1` - start x-coordinates from 1
- `smooth` - add to make a smooth curve instead of line segments
- `mark=*` - show markers at data points

